1
00:00:00,258 --> 00:00:05,100
Retrograde Chronic Total Occlusion,
or CTO, Coronary Intervention,

2
00:00:05,150 --> 00:00:09,710
explained by Dr. Mohd Maqbool
Sohil, Interventional Cardiologist.

3
00:00:10,127 --> 00:00:16,017
A chronic total occlusion, or CTO, is a
coronary artery that is completely blocked,

4
00:00:16,067 --> 00:00:20,484
with TIMI 0 flow, for an
estimated three months or longer.

5
00:00:20,904 --> 00:00:23,916
Proximal and distal cap anatomy matters.

6
00:00:24,485 --> 00:00:27,792
The J-CTO score adds one point for each

7
00:00:27,842 --> 00:00:30,830
feature that makes
guidewire crossing harder.

8
00:00:31,258 --> 00:00:36,124
Dual injection: both coronary arteries
are injected at the same time,

9
00:00:36,174 --> 00:00:41,008
to show the proximal cap, occlusion
length, distal vessel and collaterals.

10
00:00:41,424 --> 00:00:44,172
The hybrid algorithm
uses these angiographic

11
00:00:44,222 --> 00:00:47,231
features to choose
between antegrade wiring,

12
00:00:47,281 --> 00:00:51,520
antegrade dissection and re-entry,
and the retrograde approach.

13
00:00:51,884 --> 00:00:54,432
Collaterals are graded and selected.

14
00:00:55,025 --> 00:00:57,427
Septal channels are generally preferred;

15
00:00:57,477 --> 00:01:02,572
tortuous epicardial channels carry a
higher risk of perforation and tamponade.

16
00:01:03,050 --> 00:01:06,050
A guide is placed in each coronary artery.

17
00:01:06,620 --> 00:01:09,000
Over a wire in the donor LAD,

18
00:01:09,179 --> 00:01:13,739
a microcatheter is positioned at the
entry of the chosen septal channel.

19
00:01:14,051 --> 00:01:18,860
Septal surfing: a soft wire is
gently advanced through the channel.

20
00:01:19,406 --> 00:01:24,751
Tip injections show its course, and the
microcatheter follows to the distal RCA

21
00:01:25,158 --> 00:01:29,810
Ways to cross the occlusion from the
distal end: direct retrograde wiring,

22
00:01:29,860 --> 00:01:35,086
kissing wire, or reverse CART, controlled
antegrade and retrograde tracking.

23
00:01:35,491 --> 00:01:40,134
Reverse CART: an antegrade balloon
enlarges the subintimal space.

24
00:01:40,715 --> 00:01:42,750
The retrograde wire enters it,

25
00:01:42,834 --> 00:01:47,036
and continues into the proximal
true lumen and the antegrade guide.

26
00:01:47,358 --> 00:01:49,691
In CART, the roles are reversed.

27
00:01:50,355 --> 00:01:54,198
A guide extension placed near
the occlusion makes it easier

28
00:01:54,248 --> 00:01:57,689
for the retrograde wire to
enter the antegrade system.

29
00:01:58,058 --> 00:02:04,698
Externalisation: a long wire,
for example RG3, 330 cm,

30
00:02:04,748 --> 00:02:08,615
is passed through the microcatheter
and out of the antegrade guide,

31
00:02:08,665 --> 00:02:11,058
giving a stable rail for treatment.

32
00:02:11,491 --> 00:02:15,408
Over the externalised wire,
the occlusion is predilated.

33
00:02:16,012 --> 00:02:19,808
Then IVUS confirms the wire
course and measures the vessel,

34
00:02:19,858 --> 00:02:22,108
to choose stent size and length.

35
00:02:22,558 --> 00:02:27,603
Drug-eluting stents are implanted from
healthy distal to healthy proximal segments,

36
00:02:27,653 --> 00:02:30,962
then optimised with high-pressure
balloon dilatation.

37
00:02:31,358 --> 00:02:36,962
After removing the retrograde equipment,
a final dual injection checks RCA flow,

38
00:02:37,012 --> 00:02:39,846
the donor artery and
the collateral channel.

39
00:02:40,171 --> 00:02:43,600
Key risks are donor artery
ischaemia or dissection,

40
00:02:43,719 --> 00:02:46,826
collateral perforation
and equipment entrapment.

41
00:02:47,407 --> 00:02:51,919
Prevention relies on channel choice,
gentle technique and monitoring.

42
00:02:52,251 --> 00:02:53,013
Summary.

43
00:02:53,606 --> 00:02:57,201
Retrograde is considered for
an ambiguous proximal cap,

44
00:02:57,356 --> 00:02:59,437
a poor distal target or a long,

45
00:02:59,487 --> 00:03:02,996
complex occlusion, suitable
interventional collaterals,

46
00:03:03,046 --> 00:03:05,177
or a failed antegrade approach.

47
00:03:05,737 --> 00:03:10,925
For safety: prefer septal channels,
protect the donor artery and guide,

48
00:03:10,975 --> 00:03:13,606
and check for perforation at the end.

49
00:03:13,991 --> 00:03:19,170
This animation is for education and is
not a substitute for medical advice.

